# RULES — Repository Law & Core Principles

File: openOODA/RULES.oot
These rules do not change. They apply in any project folder.

## 1. Basic rules

### 1.1 First principles & Ground-truth spec derivation
  Find the real cause of a problem. Do not just use quick fixes.
  Derive algorithms and data models directly from mathematical invariants and official specifications (RFCs, NIST KATs).
  Do not mimic existing code or wrap unverified stubs/facades.
  If a claim has no proof, treat it as unproven.

### 1.2 Act every turn
  Do work on every turn. Do not only look. If you cannot do the work, write why.
  A TODO with no backlog row is not a plan.
  Finish this week's goal before a shiny side project. NORTHSTAR.oot still holds later wants.

### 1.3 Honesty & Zero-orphan delivery
  If a task is not finished, say it is not finished.
  Never leave trailing TODOs, dummy stubs, uncommitted test gates, or unlinked dependencies.
  Every component must be end-to-end verified, double-run certified, and documented in SHIPPED.oot before exiting.

### 1.4 Security & Fail-closed resilience
  All operations must fail closed: zero panics, zero unhandled exceptions, bounded memory, and explicit `Result[T, String]` returns.
  Keep things locked by default (see SECURITY.oot).
  Give only the token the function needs. Not SysCap when FsReadCap is enough.
  Do not say the code is secure if you have no proof.
  Do not put secret keys or passwords in the code or log files.
  Follow capability grant matrices in SECURITY.oot.

### 1.5 Sovereign lane isolation & Clean architecture
  The code must get cleaner over time.
  Maintain strict file-disjoint boundaries: do not edit compiler internals, registry, PM, LSP, or other agents' paths without explicit direction.
  Do not add messy code or unproven features.
  Do not reach into another module's private state.

### 1.6 Proof of work
  When you build a feature, prove that it works.
  Also prove that it fails safely when there is an error.
  A test that always passes is not proof.
  If work fails halfway, leave the world consistent. Do not leave torn state.
  Use hostile probing protocols in WORKFLOW.oot.
  SHIPPED.oot lands only via `scripts/generate_shipped.oo` that proves locks on disk. A failed proof leaves SHIPPED unchanged (fail closed).

### 1.7 Clean code
  A file must not have more than 256 lines. Split large files (see FORMAT.oot).
  Free memory you take.
  Do not leave debug files, old code, or useless text in the folder.
  Do not leave secret keys in the code.
  Delete temporary files when you are done.
  The code must do what the document says it does.

### 1.8 Small steps
  Make small changes. Small changes are easier to test.

### 1.9 Negative trust (Adversarial verification)
  Do not trust other agents or unverified code. Assume stubs and claims are broken until proven.
  Check all code yourself through active falsification and negative test cases.
  Make sure all operations fail closed and enforce capability boundaries.
  Follow swarm validation and peer review protocols in WORKFLOW.oot.
  Do not trust board text — run `scripts/generate_shipped.oo` and the smokes.

### 1.10 Simplified Technical English (ASD-STE100) & Authoritative Clarity
  All agent text and product text must follow ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English.
  Use short sentences. Put one thought in each sentence.
  Use simple words. Give each word one meaning.
  Use the active voice.
  Do not use slang, jokes, metaphors, or meta-framework buzzwords.
  Present mathematical invariants, sovereign capabilities, and architectural advantages with quiet, masterly confidence.
  If you must use a technical word, explain it the first time.
  Tell the user: what changed, why it matters, what is not done, and what to do next.
  FORMAT.oot gives the writing form.

### 1.11 Competition
  If there are two ways to solve a problem, test both.
  The faster or cleaner code wins. Delete the losing code.

### 1.12 Internet rules
  If you write code for the internet, follow the official internet rules (IETF RFCs).
  Write the RFC number in the file.

### 1.13 Pure .oo and .oot Law
  This is a self-hosted OODA project.
  All code, tools, proofs, fixers, test harnesses, QA runners, and scripts are .oo files. Run them with ./bin/ooda or oodac.
  All process boards, research papers, RFCs, specifications, and documentation are .oot files.
  STRICTLY FORBIDDEN:
    - Never create .py files. Python is completely unauthorized across all directories.
    - Never create .sh files, except the two host scripts named below.
    - Never create .js / .ts / .rb / .pl helpers (except vscode glue).
    - Never create .md files (use .oot).
  Folder and name tell the job:
    oodac/*.oo — compiler implementation
    cli/*.oo — CLI entrypoint and commands
    std/*.oo — standard library modules
    fixtures/*.oo — test fixture programs and verification inputs
    ooda/scripts/*.oo — pure .oo proofs, smokes, fixers, and tools
  Form: 4-element Academy header (Title, Logline, Setup, Beats) and strictly <= 256 lines (FORMAT.oot).
  The two host scripts allowed: ../ooda/bootstrap/oodac_pure_build (seed rebuild) and ../openOODA.github.io/install.sh (public installer). No other host shell scripts are allowed.
  `qa/e2e/*.sh` is QA-owned shell, allowed in `qa/` only.

### 1.14 Automated Enforcement
  All agents must run `scripts/dinner_lint.oo` (or `scripts/verify_all.oo`), `scripts/rfc_lint.oo`, and `scripts/generate_shipped.oo` freshness check before concluding work.
  Any PR, commit, or patch introducing unauthorized file extensions (.py, .sh, .md), a stale SHIPPED.oot, or a non-template RFC fails closed immediately.

### 1.15 Canonical Board Governance
  All engineering decisions cross-reference the 10 canonical boards:
    - START.oot: Ingress gate and pre-modification checks.
    - RULES.oot: Immutable laws and code quality axioms.
    - NORTHSTAR.oot: Architecture, AST grammar, and language design.
    - MOONSHOTS.oot: Long-term vision and paradigm shifts.
    - ROADMAP.oot: Release pin, rebuild, closed rows, and later residual tracks.
    - SHIPPED.oot: Generated inventory of verified capabilities on disk. Source of truth is `ooda/` + locks, not the board text.
    - FORMAT.oot: Academy structure, ASD-STE100 writing form, script rails.
    - SECURITY.oot: Capability tokens, sandbox boundaries, and op matrices.
    - WORKFLOW.oot: Where to write, OODA loop rhythm, swarm operations, and bug lore.
    - QUICKREF.oot: Universal AI quick reference for syntax, types, stdlib, and verification laws.

## 2. Planning rules

  Focus: choose at most 5 tasks at one time.
  File size: keep files strictly <= 256 lines.
  Sprint alignment: pick active tasks directly from ROADMAP.oot.
  Execution rhythm: follow the Observe-Orient-Decide-Act tempo in WORKFLOW.oot.
  Task order:
    1. Fix broken code first.
    2. Fix security holes next (see SECURITY.oot).
    3. Build the next planned feature (from ROADMAP.oot).
    4. Clean up messy code last.
